        body: 'Ms. Delores Banks taught the middle-school class at Zion Hill Baptist, and she had a pile of red cardboard bricks from the vacation Bible school stage. One Sunday she stacked them into a wall across the front of the room, tall enough that the boys in the back could barely see over it. "This wall," she said, "is your mind. Behind it is everything Yahweh is building in you." She asked Jaylen to come up and be the enemy. He grinned and tried to walk through. The wall held. "Now," she said, "every time we look at something we know is wrong, or listen to something we know is dirty, a brick comes out." She pulled one brick. Then another. "One look does not seem like much. But keep pulling." By the sixth brick Jaylen stepped right through the gap, and the whole class laughed. Then it got quiet, because they understood. Ms. Banks asked them where bricks get pulled. Phones, said Amari. Videos that pop up, said Nia. The group chat, said somebody in the back, and everybody looked at him, and he shrugged. "So how do we put the bricks back?" Ms. Banks asked. They worked it out together. Tell a grown-up when something bad shows up on a screen; do not hide it. Close it right away. Fill your eyes and ears with good things. Pray and ask Jesus to help, because He forgives and He makes us clean. Each time someone gave an answer, they put a brick back. When the wall was whole again she read them a verse a king once wrote: "I will set no wicked thing before mine eyes" (Psalms 101:3). She gave each child one cardboard brick to take home. Nia put hers on her bedroom shelf next to her tablet. When her cousin sent her a link she did not like, she looked at the brick, closed the link, and went to find her mom.',
