        'Many of us raised children before the internet, and some of us raised them as it arrived. Pastor Stephen Darby of Destined Ministries in Louisville preached a sermon on pornography that Next Chapter Radio played on 17 January 2016, and much of it was aimed at people our age: parents and grandparents who handed the phone to a child to keep the peace. He began without hedging. At 4:27 he says, "This is the most destructive thing that any culture has ever accepted." His argument, in summary, ran like this. When he was young, he said, a boy had to search hard to find such material, and might find one magazine in two years. Now, at 7:00, "we got a generation of young men who grew up on it," because children can get past the blocks on a computer. A child cannot yet tell fantasy from reality, so at 8:20 he concludes that "most men that you meet learn sex wrong," and spend their lives chasing a rush instead of learning intimacy and love. He said what pornography does to the mind, at 8:53, "is make everything an object of your desire or the object of your lust." At 57:07 he puts it this way: it "trains you not to see the person but see the object of your release." He did not say this was only a men’s sin. He named women as well. Then he went to the Word. He read James 1: a person is tempted "when he is drawn away of his own lust, and enticed" (James 1:14), and then "Then when lust hath conceived, it bringeth forth sin: and sin, when it is finished, bringeth forth death." (James 1:15). He argued that temptation works through a weakness already present, and that the adversary looks for that weakness the way the Philistines looked for Samson’s. He pictured the mind as a wall. Each act of giving in removes a brick, until, at 37:11, "I’ve created an opening for the enemy to come in." That, he said, is what addiction is: meaning it when you promise to stop and lacking the power to do it. The proverb he leaned on fits: "He that hath no rule over his own spirit is like a city that is broken down, and without walls." (Proverbs 25:28). And the wages come in this life. At 40:04 he says, "death always means loss": marriages, children, livelihoods and good names. He read research findings, published in a 2009 report, that married men who use pornography feel less satisfied with and less attached to their wives, and that wives notice and are hurt by the difference. He set it all under Jesus’ own words: "But I say unto you, That whosoever looketh on a woman to lust after her hath committed adultery with her already in his heart." (Matthew 5:28) At 46:03 he says, "God is after the heart." His call to action was concrete, and much of it is for us. At 80:45 he says, "God gonna hold you accountable for what you let in your house." At 82:55 he tells parents to "stop allowing the culture to dictate your house," to know what is on a child’s phone, and to hold the standard high even if we fell short ourselves at their age. To husbands and wives he said: no locked passwords between you. To men he said: admit you cannot beat this alone. He told how, in his strong younger years, he kept everything inside and leaned on no one, and only learned to reach for help and for his wife when his strength failed. There is a word in this sermon especially for our generation. He described a grandmother who came up through Jim Crow, segregation and long days working in other people’s houses, biting her tongue to feed her babies, and whose faith grew mature and patient through that struggle. Then, he said, because she knew how hard it had been, she resolved that her children would never go through what she went through, and gave them everything. The children grew up without struggle, and their faith, never tested, was weak; some looked down on the very religion that had fed them. He reached for Paul’s picture of the heir who, while a child, is kept under tutors like a servant until he is ready for the inheritance (Galatians 4:1-2). His point was not to fault our love but to say that a faith never tested is easily breached, and that the adversary looks for the generation whose walls were never built. We cannot hand our grandchildren our struggle, but we can hand them our testimony, our standards and our prayers. And we can hold the bar high, as he urged, even where we ourselves once fell: a parent who failed at that age should still say to a child, in effect, I was down here, but for you it is going to be up here. Weighed by the Word, he stands on solid ground. Jesus did place the sin in the heart. James does trace the chain from lust to death. And Paul gives the remedy he preached: "be ye transformed by the renewing of your mind" (Romans 12:2). Where he spoke of weaknesses passed down from parents, the Word holds both truths: children feel the fruit of their parents’ sins, yet each soul answers for its own, and the pattern can be broken in Christ. Our part as elders is to guard our own eyes, to guard the house, and to make it safe for a grandchild to tell us the truth, so that confession, not hiding, becomes the family habit. And over all of it stands the promise: "If we confess our sins, he is faithful and just to forgive us our sins, and to cleanse us from all unrighteousness." (1 John 1:9)',
