        title: 'Two Witnesses',
        body: 'The group chat for the youth ministry at Bethel AME in Memphis lit up on a Tuesday night. DeShawn had posted a video: a man with a deep voice and dramatic music, explaining that a famous company was secretly poisoning the water in Black neighborhoods, and that "they" had planned it a hundred years ago. Forty-one people watched it. Twelve shared it. Somebody’s aunt had already put it on Facebook. Pastor Terrell Hughes saw it at ten o’clock and did not answer right away. On Wednesday night he played the first two minutes of it for the youth group. Then he stopped it. "Before we talk about the water," he said, "let me ask: who made this video, and how do they know?" Nobody knew. The account was three weeks old. The man never said his name. "Pastor Darby used to say you need to know what you are watching," he said. "He was talking about the TV news. But the rule works on every screen, including this one." He wrote a verse on the whiteboard: "He that is first in his own cause seemeth just; but his neighbour cometh and searcheth him." (Proverbs 18:17). "The first story always sounds right," he said. "Then somebody checks it." So they checked it, right there, together. Kiana found the city’s water reports, which were public and posted every year. Malik found that a real lawsuit had been filed against the water company two years before, over old lead pipes in some neighborhoods, and that the city had agreed to replace them. "So something was wrong," DeShawn said slowly. "Just not what the video said." "Exactly," said Pastor Hughes. "There was a real problem with real pipes, and real people fought it in court and won. The video took that truth, wrapped it in a story nobody can check, and sold you fear. If you believe the fake part, you might miss the real fight." DeShawn deleted his post. Then he did something better. He posted the link to the city’s pipe replacement map and wrote: "Check if your street is on here. Mine is." Thirty people looked. Two families found out their homes qualified. The next Sunday, Pastor Hughes taught the youth one more verse: Jesus said, take heed that no man deceive you. "That includes the man with the deep voice," he said, and they laughed.',
