        title: 'What My Father Watched',
        body: 'Brenda Coleman buried her father, Deacon James Coleman, in March, and in April she finally cleaned out his den in Gary, Indiana. The television was still on the same channel. Next to his recliner were stacks of notebooks. She expected sermon notes. Instead she found years of evening news written down by hand: the wars, the elections, the prices of gas. On a page from March 2003 he had written in block capitals, THEY SAY HE HAS WEAPONS. MY GRANDSON SHIPS OUT IN MAY. Brenda sat down on the floor. Her nephew Kevin had gone to Iraq that year and come home different. She remembered her father defending the war at Thanksgiving because the news had been so sure. Further down the stack she found a page from 2006, in a shakier hand: PRESIDENT SAYS THE INTELLIGENCE WAS WRONG. WHO ANSWERS FOR KEVIN? There was nothing else written on that page. Brenda thought about the last ten years of her father’s life. He had grown bitter, then suspicious of everything, then he had started forwarding her videos about secret plots, one wilder than the next, and she had stopped opening them. He had swung from believing every anchor to believing every stranger. In the bottom notebook, the last one, she found something different. He had copied out a verse in his best handwriting: "And Jesus answered and said unto them, Take heed that no man deceive you." (Matthew 24:4). Under it he had written, I trusted the box too much. Then I trusted the voices that hated the box too much. Lord, teach me to trust You and check everything else. Brenda took that page to her Bible study the next week. She told the women about the notebooks, about Kevin, about the forwarded videos. "Daddy wasn’t foolish," she said. "He wanted to know the truth. He just looked for it in the wrong places, first one way and then the other." The women prayed with her. Then they agreed on a practice: before any of them shared a news story or a video, they would ask two questions out loud. Who is telling me this, and how do they know? And what does the Word say about how I should respond? Brenda keeps her father’s last page in her Bible, at Matthew 24.',
