        title: 'Watch Party',
        body: 'Every year the girls in the Thursday youth group at New Jerusalem Church of God in Christ in Detroit held a watch party for the big music awards. Pizza, matching pajamas, scorecards for the best outfits. This year Jasmine, who was fifteen and in charge of the scorecards, said she wanted to try something different. "Let’s not just watch it," she said. "Let’s weigh it." Her grandmother had played her an old sermon by a preacher from Louisville who said the industry makes idols and then gives them a message. Jasmine thought that was a little dramatic. But she wanted to test it. So the scorecards had new columns: What is this song telling me to do? What is the stage telling me to worship? Would I sing this in front of Jesus? The first performance was a ballad, and it was beautiful, and the girls gave it high marks. The second was a dance number, and halfway through Brianna said, "Wait, read the lyrics on the screen," and they all got quiet. They had been dancing to it for months. The third was staged with smoke and candles and a lot of symbols nobody could name, and the girls who had been yelling for it a minute before were suddenly arguing about what it meant. By the end of the night the scorecards were full of crossings-out. Not everything was bad. Two songs were genuinely good, one about a mother’s love and one about getting through a hard year. But Jasmine noticed something she wrote down on the back of her card: the songs that pulled hardest on her were the ones she had never once thought about. Before they went to sleep, the youth leader, Sister Patrice, asked them to sing one song together with no music track at all. They chose an old hymn their grandmothers knew. It sounded thin at first. Then Brianna took the harmony and it filled the room. Sister Patrice read them a verse before lights out: "Little children, keep yourselves from idols. Amen." (1 John 5:21). "Music is not the enemy," she said. "Yahweh made it. The question is who it is pointing you to." Jasmine kept her scorecard. Next year she wants to add one more column.',
