        title: 'But If Not',
        body: 'Raymond Hollis had played keyboard for nineteen years: in church on Sundays and, for the last ten, in a well-known touring band during the week. The money paid for his mother’s care and his daughter’s tuition at Tuskegee. In the spring the band’s manager called a meeting. The new tour would open with a stage show the designers had been working on for a year, and Raymond saw the sketches: a huge golden figure rising from the stage floor, the audience lit to look as if they were bowing toward it, lyrics on the screen that mocked prayer. "It’s just theater," the manager said. "Nobody takes it seriously." Raymond took it seriously. That night he could not sleep. He thought about Peter following Jesus at a distance, warming himself at the fire in the high priest’s courtyard, saying he did not know Him. He had been warming himself at that fire for ten years, telling himself it was only a job. He prayed until three in the morning, and then he read Daniel 3 again, the three young men in front of the king’s image. He had always loved the part about the fourth man in the fire. This time he could not get past the line before it: "But if not, be it known unto thee, O king, that we will not serve thy gods, nor worship the golden image which thou hast set up." (Daniel 3:18) But if not. He went to the manager the next day and said he could not play the opening. He offered to play the rest of the show, or to help find his replacement. The manager was not unkind, but he was clear: the opening was the show. Raymond lost the tour. The next months were hard. His wife took extra shifts. His pastor quietly helped with his mother’s bills. He took students, then a small studio job, then a position directing music at a growing church across town that paid less than a third of the old money. One Sunday his daughter came home from school and sat in the back of that church. She told him afterward that when the choir sang she had seen him crying at the keyboard, and she wanted to know why. "Because I am not bowing anymore," he said. "And because He met me in the fire."',
