        title: 'The Celebrity Sunday',
        body: 'Pastor Lionel Grant of True Vine Missionary Baptist in Houston got the call on a Monday: a famous comedian, recently baptized, wanted to give his testimony at True Vine on Sunday. The man’s manager promised a sold-out crowd and a camera crew. The church’s finance committee was thrilled; the roof needed work. Pastor Grant was less sure. He had heard the man’s jokes, and he had read his latest book, which told young women how to manage men rather than how to honor Yahweh. He did not doubt that the man could be saved. He doubted that a pulpit was the place to start. So he called the comedian himself. They talked for an hour. The man was more honest on the phone than on stage. He said he was new at this, that he had a lot to unlearn, and that his manager wanted the church crowd for his next tour. Pastor Grant laughed and said he appreciated the honesty. Then he made an offer. "Come on Sunday," he said. "Sit on the second row with me. Don’t speak. Let the church love on you. Then come to our men’s Bible study on Tuesdays for a few months. When you have something the Word has taught you, not something your manager wrote, I will give you the microphone myself." The manager was not happy. The finance committee was less happy. But the comedian showed up on Sunday and sat on the second row and cried during the altar call. He kept coming on Tuesdays, mostly listening. Six months later he stood up in the men’s group and said he wanted to take back some of what he had written in his book, and that he did not know how to do it publicly without losing everything. The men prayed over him. A year later he spoke at True Vine for the first time. It was short. He talked about being a wolf who did not know he was one, and about a pastor who loved him enough to say not yet. The roof got fixed the next year, without a camera crew. Pastor Grant preached that Sunday from Matthew 7, where Jesus said "Ye shall know them by their fruits" (Matthew 7:16).',
