        title: 'The Wednesday Night Empty Room',
        body: 'Mother Ernestine Walker had kept the key to the prayer room at Mount Olive Church of God in Baltimore for twenty-two years. Wednesday nights at seven she unlocked it, set out the folding chairs, and waited. When she started there were forty chairs and not enough. Now she set out eight and filled three. Everybody had a reason. Bible study had moved online. The young families had ball practice. The men’s league played on Wednesdays. Somebody had told her the big new show everyone was watching aired at eight on Wednesdays, and she had laughed, and then she had stopped laughing. One night in March her great-nephew Andre, who was nineteen and home from college, came to pick her up and found her sitting alone in the prayer room with the lights low. She was not crying. She was praying out loud, naming streets: Greenmount, North Avenue, Monument. She was naming young men she had buried. She was naming the city council, the mayor, the President, the police commissioner, one by one. Andre sat down in the back without meaning to. He had spent the whole semester arguing about politics online. He had opinions about every candidate. He had never once prayed for any of them. When she finished she looked at him and said, "You know why the enemy keeps folks busy on Wednesdays, baby? Because he knows what happens when they come." She handed him her worn Bible open to 2 Chronicles: "If my people, which are called by my name, shall humble themselves, and pray, and seek my face, and turn from their wicked ways; then will I hear from heaven, and will forgive their sin, and will heal their land." (2 Chronicles 7:14) Andre came back the next Wednesday. He brought two friends from his old basketball team. The Wednesday after that there were nine. They did not fix the city. But in June, after a shooting on Monument Street, the families did not have to look for a church to pray with them. The prayer room was already full, and the lights were on.',
