        body: 'Every Thanksgiving the Jefferson family in Atlanta did a thousand-piece jigsaw puzzle on Grandma Lillian’s dining table. This year Uncle Curtis, who had become very interested in what he called the hidden plan, brought one without a box. "Just the pieces," he said. "That’s how life is. They give you pieces and hide the picture." For two hours the family struggled. Cousins argued over whether a blue piece was sky or water. Little Zoe kept putting a brown piece in the wrong corner. Uncle Curtis said that proved his point: without the box top, you could never see what was really going on. Then Grandma Lillian came out of the kitchen with her apron on and her Bible under her arm. She sat down, opened it, and said, "The box top is right here, and it has been here the whole time." She read them a verse: "For we wrestle not against flesh and blood, but against principalities, against powers, against the rulers of the darkness of this world, against spiritual wickedness in high places." (Ephesians 6:12) "The pieces are people," she said. "Politicians, news anchors, rich folks, poor folks. The picture is a war bigger than any of them. If you think the enemy is your neighbor, you will spend your life fighting the wrong pieces." Uncle Curtis grinned. "So you agree there is a plan." "Of course there is a plan," she said. "And there is a King whose plan wins. Read the last page." Everyone laughed, even Curtis. Then she put on her glasses, found the four corner pieces, and showed the children how to build from the edges in. By the time the pie came out, the picture was half done: a lighthouse on a dark coast, its beam cutting across the water. Zoe found the last piece of the beam herself. Grandma Lillian made them all hold hands around the table before dessert, and instead of the usual grace she asked each person to pray for one leader by name. Uncle Curtis prayed for the President. It was the first time anyone could remember him doing that.',
